WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 126: William Henry Harrison and the Log Cabin Campaign: The Curse of Tippecanoe

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.200
[Rebecca] Today, we meet America's ninth president, William Henry Harrison.

4
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.440
[Rebecca] Harrison holds two extraordinary records in the American presidency.

5
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:24.320
[Rebecca] He was elected in the wildest, most raucous campaign ever conceived, and he served the shortest term of any president in American history.

6
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:35.360
[Rebecca] In 1840, the Whig Party transformed a 67-year-old general into a frontier myth with log cabins and hard cider, winning the White House by a landslide.

7
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.920
[Rebecca] But 31 days after taking the oath, a freezing wind took his life.

8
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:47.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the log cabin campaign and the curse of Tippicano.

9
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.960
[George] In late 1839, the Whig Party was desperate to capture the presidency.

10
00:00:52.960 --> 00:01:01.520
[George] The nation was reeling under the panic of 1837, but the Whigs had been repeatedly outmaneuvered by Democratic organizational discipline.

11
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:13.600
[George] Bypassing their brilliant party leader, Henry Clay of Kentucky, the Whigs decided they needed a military hero, an older, non-ideological figure who had no controversial voting record.

12
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:24.400
[George] They chose 67-year-old General William Henry Harrison, who had defeated Tecumseh's tribal Confederacy at the Battle of Tippecano nearly 30 years earlier in 1811.

13
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:36.320
[George] Shortly after Harrison's nomination, a partisan Democratic newspaper in Baltimore, the Baltimore Republican, published a smug, dismissive editorial written by a supporter of Henry Clay.

14
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:51.520
[George] The journalist sneered, give him a barrel of hard cider, and settle a pension of 2,000 a year on him, and my word for it, he will sit the remainder of his days in his log cabin on the banks of the Ohio and never care to be president.

15
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.760
[George] It was the most catastrophic political insult in American history.

16
00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.440
[George] Instead of apologizing, the Whigs brilliantly seized the slur and turned it into the central symbol of the campaign.

17
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:14.600
[George] In a nation where millions of pioneer families had been born in log cabins and drank hard cider on the frontier, the Democratic jab was seen as an arrogant insult against ordinary working Americans.

18
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:22.840
[George] Whigs strategists instantly transformed William Henry Harrison into the saint of the log cabin and the apostle of the hard cider barrel.

19
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:29.640
[George] The image of William Henry Harrison as an impoverished backwoods frontiersman was a masterpiece of political fiction.

20
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:37.480
[George] In reality, Harrison was born into the highest tier of the Virginia planter aristocracy at Berkeley Plantation on the James River.

21
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:44.920
[George] His father, Benjamin Harrison V, was a signer of the Declaration of Independence and a governor of Virginia.

22
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:58.680
[George] Harrison himself lived on a prosperous 3,000-acre estate at North Bend, Ohio, residing not in a rustic cabin, but in an elegant, 22-room clapboard mansion complete with imported carpets and fine china.

23
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.240
[George] None of that mattered in 1840.

24
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:11.160
[George] Whigs pointed out that at the center of Harrison's mansion sat the original four-room log cabin he had built decades earlier, which had merely been encased in modern siding.

25
00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.640
[George] That was enough for campaign mythmakers.

26
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.880
[George] Overnight, log cabins sprouted across the American landscape.

27
00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:32.680
[George] In every city and village from Boston to Cincinnati, Whig clubs erected real log cabins on wooden wagons, complete with coonskin caps nailed to the door, smoke curling from stone chimneys, and live raccoons tethered to the porch.

28
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:39.960
[George] Barrels of hard cider were tapped at every rally, distributing free alcohol to anyone willing to cheer for Harrison.

29
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.960
[George] A Philadelphia distiller named E.

30
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:42.200
[George] C.

31
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:57.280
[George] Booze produced whiskey and amber glass bottles shaped like log cabins, popularizing the slang term booze, while incumbent President Martin Van Buren was smeared as an effeminate dandy who scented his whiskers with cologne and drank champagne.

32
00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:03.920
[George] Harrison was hailed as the rugged farmer who worked his own fields and drank the honest cider of the common man.

33
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:15.040
[George] The 1840 campaign was the first to realize that songs, slogans, and visual spectacle were vastly more effective at mobilizing voters than dry policy manifestos.

34
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:23.040
[George] Whigs wrote hundreds of catchy campaign songs, compiled into printed log cabin songbooks that sold by the tens of thousands.

35
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.800
[George] Crowds of twenty thousand gathered to sing in thunderous unison.

36
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.200
[George] What has caused this great commotion, motion, motion, our country through?

37
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:40.160
[George] It is the ball a rolling on, for Tippicano and Tyler too, and with them will beat Little Van, Van, Van, Van is a used-up man.

38
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:46.640
[George] The phrase the ball a rolling on was not a metaphor, it was a literal physical campaign phenomenon.

39
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:57.360
[George] Whig activists built colossal ten-foot-tall spheres made of canvas and leather stretched over wooden hoops, painted with patriotic slogans and political attacks on Van Buren.

40
00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:09.760
[George] Young men pushed these giant balls along dirt roads, rolling them for hundreds of miles from town to town across Maryland, Pennsylvania, and Ohio, giving the English language its permanent idiom.

41
00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.440
[George] Keep the ball rolling.

42
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.160
[George] Rallies reached astronomical proportions.

43
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:21.600
[George] In Nashville, 75,000 people gathered for a three-day barbecue, roasting dozens of oxen hole.

44
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:35.000
[George] Harrison became the first presidential candidate in American history to break tradition and actively deliver campaign speeches on his own behalf, traveling across Ohio and speaking to cheering throngs from the back of open carriages.

45
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:41.880
[George] When the votes were counted in November, voter turnout reached an astonishing 80% of eligible voters.

46
00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:47.560
[George] Harrison crushed Van Buren in the Electoral College, 234 to 60.

47
00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:54.040
[George] On March 4, 1841, William Henry Harrison arrived in Washington to claim his prize.

48
00:05:54.040 --> 00:06:03.160
[George] At age 68, he was the oldest man ever elected president up to that time, a record he would hold for over a century until Ronald Reagan.

49
00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:15.960
[George] Sensitive to Democratic whispers that he was a senile, feeble old man who would be a puppet for Henry Clay, Harrison was determined to make an overwhelming demonstration of youthful physical vigor and intellectual power.

50
00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:19.880
[George] The day dawned raw, overcast, and bitterly cold.

51
00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:29.320
[George] A freezing northeast gale swept through the Capitol, bringing bursts of icy rain, sleet, and howling winds with temperatures hovering in the low thirties.

52
00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:36.680
[George] Advisers begged Harrison to ride to the Capitol in an enclosed carriage and wear a heavy wool overcoat, hat, and gloves.

53
00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:39.000
[George] Harrison stubbornly refused.

54
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:45.880
[George] Mounting a white stallion, he rode through the freezing streets bareheaded and coatless, waving to the crowds.

55
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:54.920
[George] Standing on the open, windswept east portico of the Capitol, Harrison proceeded to deliver the longest inaugural address in American history.

56
00:06:54.920 --> 00:07:08.600
[George] Written entirely by himself and only lightly trimmed by Daniel Webster, who joked that in editing the draft he had killed seventeen Roman proconsuls, the speech ran for 8,445 words.

57
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:17.680
[George] For one hour and forty-five minutes, while the crowd huddled in freezing misery and rain soaked his clothing, Harrison read aloud from his manuscript.

58
00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:28.880
[George] When he finally concluded, his body was shivering violently, but he rode through the inaugural parade for another three hours before attending three separate inaugural balls that evening.

59
00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.600
[George] Harrison's brief tenure in the White House was a chaotic nightmare of exhaustion.

60
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:41.520
[George] Thousands of hungry Whig office seekers, desperate for government jobs after twelve years of Democratic rule, flooded the mansion.

61
00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:50.720
[George] They camped in the corridors, slept in the hallways, and followed the president into his private bedroom, thrusting letters of recommendation into his hands.

62
00:07:50.720 --> 00:08:00.480
[George] Harrison, polite to a fault, rose at five in the morning, walked to the local markets to buy groceries, and attempted to interview job applicants himself.

63
00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:07.280
[George] At the same time, Harrison found himself locked in a furious battle for executive independence against Henry Clay.

64
00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:18.080
[George] Clay, treating the aging president as a subservient figurehead, arrogantly demanded that Harrison call an immediate special session of Congress to enact Clay's legislative agenda.

65
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:25.680
[George] When Clay barged into the White House demanding cabinet appointments, Harrison looked the powerful senator in the eye and snapped.

66
00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.720
[George] Clay, you forget that I am the President of the United States.

67
00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:36.240
[George] Clay stormed out in a rage and departed for Kentucky, never to speak to Harrison again.

68
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:43.680
[George] On March 26, after 22 days in office, Harrison took his usual morning walk in a heavy, cold downpour.

69
00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.360
[George] He returned to the White House drenched and chilled to the bone.

70
00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:55.520
[George] Later that afternoon, he was seized with violent, uncontrollable chills, accompanied by severe chest pains and a burning fever.

71
00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:02.600
[George] His physicians diagnosed pneumonia and pleurisy, confining the president to his bed in the presidential suite.

72
00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:07.800
[George] In the 19th century, medical treatment was often more lethal than the disease.

73
00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:14.760
[George] Harrison's doctors subjected the frail 68-year-old president to an agonizing battery of primitive treatments.

74
00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:27.800
[George] They applied heated suction cups to his skin, placed live medicinal leeches on his chest and stomach to bleed him, and administered heavy doses of calomel, opium, castor oil, and boiled snake root.

75
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:31.240
[George] The treatments devastated Harrison's remaining strength.

76
00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.360
[George] Delirium set in as fluid filled his lungs.

77
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:42.680
[George] In moments of lucid consciousness, Harrison's mind drifted back to his political struggles with Henry Clay and his duty to the Constitution.

78
00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:49.480
[George] Gathering his last breath, he looked past his doctors to an unseen presence and whispered his final words.

79
00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:53.400
[George] Sir, I wish you to understand the true principles of the government.

80
00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.320
[George] I wish them carried out.

81
00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.080
[George] I ask nothing more.

82
00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:09.080
[George] At half past midnight on Sunday, April 4, 1841, exactly thirty days, twelve hours and thirty minutes after taking the oath of office, William Henry Harrison died.

83
00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.000
[George] He was the first president of the United States to die in office.

84
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:21.720
[George] Outside the White House gates, church bells across Washington began a solemn, rhythmic tolling that stunned the nation into grief-stricken silence.

85
00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:25.960
[George] The rollicking log cabin carnival had ended in the deepest tragedy.

86
00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:30.680
[George] Harrison's sudden death sent shockwaves through the American political system.

87
00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:37.880
[George] In popular folklore, his death gave birth to the legend of the curse of Tipicano, also known as Tecumseh's curse.

88
00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:53.520
[George] According to the legend, following Harrison's defeat of the Shawnee at Tipicano in 1811, the prophet Tensquadua, Tecumseh's brother, placed a curse upon Harrison, that every American president elected in a year ending in zero would die in office.

89
00:10:53.520 --> 00:11:12.320
[George] Remarkably, for the next 120 years, every single president elected in a zero year, Lincoln in 1860, Garfield in 1880, McKinley in 1900, Harding in 1920, Roosevelt in 1940, and Kennedy in 1960 died in office.

90
00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:16.720
[George] A macabre pattern broken only by Ronald Reagan in 1980.

91
00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.120
[George] Far more perilous than folklore was the immediate constitutional void.

92
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:38.400
[George] The United States Constitution in Article 2, Section 1, stated vaguely, in case of the removal of the president from office, or of his death, resignation, or inability to discharge the powers and duties of the said office, the same shall devolve on the vice president.

93
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.880
[George] Did the same mean the office of the president itself, or merely the powers and duties as an acting caretaker?

94
00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.320
[George] No one knew.

95
00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.400
[George] No president had ever died.

96
00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:53.120
[George] If the vice president was merely an acting caretaker, who was in charge?

97
00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:54.720
[George] Could he sign bills?

98
00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.960
[George] Could he fire cabinet officers?

99
00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.680
[George] Could new presidential elections be called?

100
00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:06.240
[George] Into this dangerous, unprecedented constitutional wilderness stepped Vice President John Tyler.

101
00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:14.480
[George] The destiny of the executive branch hung by a thread, waiting for one resolute man to define the American presidency forever.

102
00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:24.640
[Rebecca] William Henry Harrison's 31-day presidency proved that political campaigns can transform image into power, but cannot conquer mortality.

103
00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:31.240
[Rebecca] His tragic death forced the young American republic to confront the most dangerous question it had ever faced.

104
00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:34.040
[Rebecca] What happens when a president dies?

105
00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:48.840
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to America's 10th president, John Tyler, the accidental president, who stood alone against his own party and established the historic precedent that the vice president becomes the true president of the United States.

106
00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:53.800
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

